WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.910
Ever get to the end of a film, turn to your
buddy, and say, "what the hell did we just

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:04.910
watch?"

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:06.410
"In fact, never play this again."

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.700
Well, wonder no more, gentle moviegoer!

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.070
We're here to give you the scoop on the real
meanings behind these complicated movie endings...

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.400
The Matrix Revolutions

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.960
Our hero in The Matrix franchise, Neo, was
the only one with enough power to break the

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.900
Matrix and save humanity.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.880
He spent the first two films on an apparent
collision course with the near-invincible

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.560
artificial intelligence program known as Agent
Smith.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.410
But when their cataclysmic fight in the third
installment finally arrived, Neo…gave up?

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.230
So what happened at the end of The Matrix
Revolutions?

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.440
Neo lies on the brink of defeat until he realizes
he doesn't need to beat Smith at all.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.190
Neo assimilates into the system, and Smith
is wiped out while the Matrix reboots.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.010
Say, whaaat?!

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.010
"Whoa."

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.560
The dedicated fans at The Matrix 101 picked
apart the scene to figure out what really

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.350
went down, and here's what they discovered:
Neo brokered a sort of truce with the machines,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.070
allowing the continued existence of the Matrix
"free zone" known as Zion while healing the

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.470
corruption in the program personified by Smith.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:04.080
The Machines also grant every human the chance
to be unplugged from The Matrix and live in

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.310
the real world or to remain living in their
artificial reality.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.240
The essay concludes on a hopeful note:

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.439
"This is a world where eradication of the
enemy is seen for what it is: a symptom of

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.409
the problem, not a solution.

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:18.530
This is a world where the creator and its
creation have the potential to live fruitfully

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.159
in peace and cooperation."

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:22.359
The Fountain

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:26.590
Darren Aronofsky's The Fountain features three
interlocking stories, each set hundreds of

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:30.880
years apart, all about a couple coming to
terms with death — and culminates in a wild,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.880
weird ending.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.770
In one timeline, a doctor works to cure the
brain tumor of his wife, Izzi.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:39.770
She's written a book in which a Spanish conquistador
searches for the Tree of Life for his queen.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.890
Meanwhile, in the future, a cosmonaut heads
for a distant nebula in a biosphere containing

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.770
the Tree, interacting with Izzi's spirit along
the way.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.590
It all ends in the cosmonaut's fiery death,
the Tree's rebirth, and an ending in which

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:54.780
Izzi's spirit hands Tom fruit from the Tree…which
he plants in her grave.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.450
Uh, yup.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.960
It's all deeply symbolic, and anyone hoping
for a literal explanation out of The Fountain

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:01.960
will be somewhat frustrated.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:06.369
Aronofsky himself told The Washington Post
it's a "Rubik's cube" of a story that's ultimately

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.920
really about coming to grips with our own
mortality.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.319
He elaborated further in an interview with
Ain't it cool news:

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:15.860
"It's a film that's a journey and it's a trip
and it's an experience through the meditation

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.599
of a lot of these questions.

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:20.780
There are ideas in there that I believe, but
I think I wanted to leave it open, so that

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.110
anyone can bring their own beliefs to the
table, and that it could awaken them, and

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.430
people can have a conversation."

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.800
So, there you have it.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.180
Your own interpretation is the right one,
apparently.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.180
"Whoa."

00:02:31.159 --> 00:02:32.159
Interstellar

00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:36.980
Christopher Nolan's sci-fi epic Interstellar
is pretty cool...but it's also pretty confusing,

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.280
particularly in its final act.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.940
Astronaut Joseph Cooper, on a desperate mission
to find a new home for humanity, plummets

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.770
into a black hole and drifts beyond the event
horizon, finding himself inside a tesseract.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.310
There, he can see inside his daughter's bedroom
at any point in her life.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.829
He communicates with her through gravity and
Morse Code, guiding her to unlock the equation

00:02:54.829 --> 00:02:56.790
that helps humanity escape Earth.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.909
By the end, Cooper is discovered floating
through space by later members of the human

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:03.430
race he's saved, brought to meet his dying
daughter — who's aged at normal speed while

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.170
he's been on his intergalactic travels and
is thus much "older" than he is.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:07.850
Huh?

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:11.390
"Boy when it does, and it feels like they
can put a blindfold on you, and put you in

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:15.159
a spaceship and take you to Neptune, and you
can hop off on the planet, and they better

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:16.519
have the sprockets rolling..."

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:20.219
The whole film rests on the notion that time
is a circle and future humans have the ability

00:03:20.219 --> 00:03:21.239
to travel within it.

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:25.280
But they only got there by creating this tesseract
and helping past people — namely Cooper

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.159
and his daughter Murph — finish their work.

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:30.540
It's a total chicken or the egg-style paradox...but
just go with it.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.760
And while Cooper was off travelling through
the wormholes, little Murphy was living on

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.549
the surface of this circle and kicking this
whole journey into gear from a science level

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:40.959
until her dad came back to it...like he promised.

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:41.959
The Babadook

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:45.459
Figuring out The Babadook isn't that difficult
— provided you keep up with the sudden change

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:47.240
in perspective in the movie's final act.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.260
Much of this indie horror hit looks and feels
like a supernatural home invasion picture,

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.400
with the creepy Babadook creature tormenting
a single mother and her six-year-old son.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.760
But there's more going on beneath the surface.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.540
In the film's closing scenes, the Babadook
has possessed the mom and she tries to strangle

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.540
her son.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:06.349
But the boy draws the monster out of her with
a tender expression of love … and a knife

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:07.369
to the leg, of course.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:16.320
"I know you don't love me, The Babadook won't
let you, but I love you mum."

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:20.209
The boy's plan works, and the mom sends the
Babadook fleeing to the basement, where mom

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.870
has stored all mementos of her husband since
his death.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.780
That's when we realize the creature wasn't
supernatural at all — it was her years of

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.690
repressed grief, which had grown so powerful
it threatened to destroy the lives of everything

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:31.690
it touched.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:35.450
In a perfect blend of heartwarming and gross,
The Babadook's closing moments show mother

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.390
and son gathering a bowl of worms, which he
takes into the basement to feed the vanquished

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.390
beast.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:45.100
She'll always carry the monster with her because,
after all, you can't get rid of The Babadook.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.280
It Follows

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:50.700
In David Robert Mitchell's film It Follows,
a girl named Jay has a seemingly ordinary

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.700
hookup.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.190
But soon after, she's horrified to realize
she's been infected with a sexually transmitted

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:55.930
ghost.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.830
"Who you gonna call? Someone else."

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.850
The ghost takes various human forms, ranging
from someone in the victim's memories or a

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:10.080
creepy strangers, that only someone affected
with the disease can see.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.020
It constantly walks in the direction of the
latest person infected with the "DISEASE"

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.590
until it grabs them and kills them.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:18.420
Then, the ghost starts walking in the direction
of the previous person infected and continues

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:19.650
back down the line.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:23.470
You're only option is to pass it off to someone
else, hoping the chain keeps going so that

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.510
the ghost never gets to you.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:28.040
If that sounds ridiculous, well...it kinda
is.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.830
But it's the setup for a pretty devilish little
film.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:34.270
Jay learns she can only escape the evil spirit
that's trying to kill her by sleeping with

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.780
someone else to pass it on.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:40.380
Ultimately, she and her friends try killing
it — with generally unpleasant and ambiguous

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.380
results.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:45.420
After the climactic battle, Jay and her friend
Paul have sex…and later, Paul's seen driving

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.090
past a group of prostitutes.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:51.640
In the film's final shot, the duo walk down
a street while someone — or something? — follows

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:52.840
behind.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.970
Much of It Follows is open to interpretation,
and that's exactly what Mitchell wanted.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:00.310
As he's said in multiple interviews, he was
originally inspired by a nightmare in which

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.610
he knew he was being followed, knew he couldn't
get away, and knew no one could help him.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.540
As for the ambiguity of that last shot?

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:07.860
Totally intentional.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.930
Mitchell wanted viewers to decide whether
it was still following Jay and her pals or

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:11.930
not.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:13.510
Sleep tight, kids!

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.900
2001: A Space Odyssey

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.720
There are no shortage of theories about what,
exactly, was going on in Stanley Kubrick's

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.450
1968 sci-fi classic, 2001: A Space Odyssey.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:27.530
Rather than dive too far down that rabbit
hole, let's instead offer Kubrick's own assessment

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.560
of the ending, in which astronaut Dave Bowman
comes into contact with an extraterrestrial

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.550
monolith and goes through a bizarre succession
of experiences — vast space travel, seeing

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:41.580
himself at different ages, and finally being
transformed into a floating space fetus.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.160
Kubrick explained in an interview:
"In a timeless state, his life passes from

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.840
middle age to senescence to death.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:51.970
He is reborn, an enhanced being, a star child,
an angel, a superman, if you like, and returns

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:55.180
to earth prepared for the next leap forward
of man's evolutionary destiny.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.650
That is what happens on the film's simplest
level."

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.250
But what about the levels that aren't so simple?

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:04.490
Kubrick pointed out that 2001 concerns itself
with "elements of philosophy and metaphysics"

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.680
that "have nothing to do with the bare plotline."

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:10.430
So whatever his summary tells you about Bowman's
fate, you can trust there's more to it—and

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.900
that any extra meaning is entirely up to you.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.850
"I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do that."

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:18.040
He continued:

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:20.470
"The film becomes anything the viewer sees
in it.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.860
If the film stirs the emotions and penetrates
the subconscious of the viewer, if it stimulates,

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.660
however inchoately, his mythological and religious
yearnings and impulses, then it has succeeded."

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:30.710
Got all that?

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:31.710
Great.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:34.260
Just meet us down at the bar once you're done
stimulating your mythological and religious

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.520
yearnings and impulses.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.020
Or whatever.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:39.620
"D'oh!"

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.410
Thanks for watching!

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.196
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:46.880
And leave us a comment to let us know which
endings you think should've been on this list.

